From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:26:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: add 1:1 Signal voice calls through the Phone app

Registers the luna-service2 service com.palm.signal.call (call.c) when
the plug-in loads, with one handle on LuneOS's single bus, so the stock
Phone app rings for incoming Signal calls and can place outgoing ones.
The account binds to it at login and lets go of it in presage_close,
hanging up a call it still had.

call.c keeps the call state the Phone app's callStateQuery sees and
handles dial, answer and disconnect. When a call becomes active it
reports it to audiod with phone/CallStatusUpdate, a voice-only line
with transport com.palm.signal and a string call id, and sends
{"lines":[]} when it ends. It does not set an audiod scenario: audiod
starts the call on the earpiece. Events of another call than the one on
the card, and ends of calls no longer on it, are ignored.

The Rust side handles the RingRTC CallMessages (Offer, Answer,
IceUpdate, Hangup, Busy) and runs the signal-call-media engine for
each call (call_bridge.rs). The engine is its own recipe, built from
luneos-synergy-connectors im/signal/calling/media; its path comes from
SIGNAL_MEDIA_ENGINE at build time (default
/usr/libexec/signal-call-media). call_media.rs encodes and decodes the
RingRTC opaque blobs, and ice.rs fetches the STUN/TURN relays from
/v2/calling/relays through presage's Manager::identified_push_service(),
which a separate presage patch makes public. They are fetched at login
and again when their credentials are due (half the ttl the server
gives, or an hour), checked before an outgoing call and when an Offer
arrives, after the phone has started ringing. A failed fetch keeps the
previous relays. Both incoming and outgoing engines get the relays
before START, IPv6 relays included.

The engine for an incoming call starts on the Offer, so ICE is ready
when the user answers, but the engine records nothing until then;
answering sends it ACCEPT, which opens the microphone and sends
RingRTC's Accepted. If the engine could not
start, answering ends the call and hangs up on the caller. A call that
cannot be placed (number not a stored contact, no engine, no random
bytes) ends the dialer's card instead of leaving it on "Connecting".
Keys and ICE credentials come from /dev/urandom; a call is not set up
if it cannot be read.

An Offer while another call is up (an engine still running, or an
outgoing call waiting for its Answer) is answered with Busy, as RingRTC
does, and neither rings nor starts an engine; it is noted as a missed
call once. A hangup we send is HANGUP_NORMAL without a device id, as
RingRTC sends it (Hangup::Normal carries none).

Only an incoming call nobody answered is reported as a missed call:
not one we placed, not one accepted here, and not one accepted or
declined on another of the account's devices.

A dialled number is resolved through the stored contacts only. Calling
a number that is no contact would need Contact Discovery, i.e.
presage's "cdsi" feature, which pulls in BoringSSL; it is not enabled.

serde and serde_json are added for the relays JSON (both already in
Cargo.lock through presage, so only the root package's dependency list
changes), and tokio's "time" feature for the relays timeout.

CMakeLists.txt builds call.c and links luna-service2.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
